Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:07.670 align:start position:0%
 
hi everybody  welcome back to your online graduation  so before we get started with  last week i wanted to introduce you to a  very special little book which you  should have received already or will be  receiving  hopefully very very soon in your class  day in a box package that nathan spoke  about  it's called the class anthology and in  it is a series of reflections written by  members of the class of 2020  there's some space at the front for you  to record your own thoughts and  reminisces  and many black pages at the back feed  together messages from your friends when  we're all able to be together again  this isn't how any of us imagined we  would end our time as undergrads  but my hope is that you'll be able to  relate to some of the feelings expressed  in the class anthology  and hold on to it as a memento of a  significant time in our lives  and now i have the honor  of introducing the first speech of our  program  traditionally the serious reflection is  given to commemorate the experience of  just being a yearly  our four years here were you know full  of ups and downs  which makes a serious reflection all the  more important as the speaker challenges  us to find meaning  in our experiences at yale in spite of  all the differences that we might have  had  and all the challenges we may have faced  so it is with  great happiness that i am proud to  present  the serious reflection this year given  by joy chu  of ezra styles  hi class of 2020 we're graduating this  week  not in a way that we ever expected but  we are graduating nonetheless  there will be no frantic packing of bags  there will be no final tearful goodbyes  no one is going to walk across any stage  but in six days time we will nonetheless  officially become a part of yale's  history trading in our identities  as current students to emerge as  graduates of the yale college class of  2020  in these unprecedented times in the  complete absence of traditions we hold  dear what does it really mean  to graduate  in more ways than just physically covet  19 has uprooted our class  it disintegrated our expectations for a  joyous and bittersweet senior spring  it dissolved a sense of constancy and  stability we never even knew we were  taking for granted  for many of us this pandemic has  introduced uncertainty  hardship and tragedy into our lives  without diminishing the gravity of the  situation i find  comfort in the fact that our time at  yale means  more than the 1 16 that coronavirus  stole  it's hard not to let the ending color  the journey  but these past few weeks cannot and  should not fundamentally change  what yale means to us and what it has  meant to us for the better part of the  past  four years and what  does yale mean the onus is on us  now more than ever to reflect and find  what matters  what does it mean to be a yeli  what brings our class together beyond  the emotional bond  forged from the pain of loss what do we  all share  my answer not as much as you might think  admittedly we're all human and now more  educated and privileged  but the idea that we're unified or  similar in some  fundamentally yale way  is a myth  let me tell you a story about the aacc  the asian american cultural center  the acc is a place i met some of my very  best friends where i spent countless  hours planning events for the chinese  american students association  the aacc is a place i found new ways to  celebrate my identity  at lunar ball and hop hot night eating  sticky rice and writing calligraphy  without a doubt the aacc has been one of  the most integral parts of my yale  experience  at the end of my junior year i like many  of you decided to join a senior society  as is customary at initiation we all  gathered around in a circle and shared  intimate parts of our lives with each  other  i chose to talk about the aacc and  everything that it's meant to me  and that night for the very first time i  met  someone who didn't know what the aacc  was  and for a moment i was speechless amazed  that this  this formative and vibrant community so  personal to me  could be completely foreign to him and  to any yeely really because to me  yale isn't yale without the aacc  but my conception of yale is just that  it's mine and just as my friend didn't  know what the aacc  is i had no idea what yale looks like  for someone  training to become a professional  athlete next year for someone who lives  close enough to drive home for a weekday  dinner  or for someone who meditates almost  every day inside the buddhist shrine in  harkness tower  and even the words we use does foot  bring to mind  inches or the appalachian trail when you  hear  zoo do you think reprinting  and when i say aacc do you think  home it's realizations like these that  make me fully appreciate the different  lives that people here lead  and it's these realizations that  convince me  believing that we all could share  something anything  real that has to be a myth  maybe it's hard to believe me when i say  that because if you picture the friends  and classmates you've been zooming  week in and week out and think about the  intersections of your lives  your shared experiences will feel so  salient and so  real i don't doubt that  there's the 10 or so people you've lived  with the 50 or 100 friends you've made  through extracurriculars  and the hundreds more you've met in  class at the gym  waiting in line at the dining hall  but the truth is there are probably  almost a thousand of you in the class of  2020  that i've never had the pleasure of  exchanging a single word with  and whatever yale means to me whatever  spaces i've inhabited  there are parts of your yale experiences  that i can't  possibly begin to conceive of  sure it means something to be a yeli  it means that in 15 years when you  encounter someone who also went to yale  you can squabble over the best  residential college or laugh about zoom  university  you can reminisce about our wins at  harvard yale or your favorite jihab  sandwich  or how pretty sterling looks at winter's  first snowfall  but the truth is aside from  participating in these same  yale traditions and sharing in some of  these more surface level memories  our lived experiences at yale are so  wildly different  and it would be a bit misguided to  believe that there is some  core quality about being a yeah that  unites us all  the truth is there isn't  this may seem depressing  but it's not this perspective is  actually kind of  uplifting because the alternative to  believing that we share  nothing in common would be to believe  that yale is so  seductive that four years here is enough  to hammer parts of our identities into  shapes completely indistinguishable from  each other  as an institution yale sells us a  version of  the good life things that we should  value things like  prestige academic excellence  professional success  productivity critical thinking and civil  discourse  these can all be good things they can  but it would be so so horrible if we all  journey through yale like sponges  absorbing these skills and values and  personality traits  so that when we emerge on the other end  we've lost the fibers of who we once  were and are  similar enough so saturated with things  that are  classically mainstream yale  that i could stand here and give a  speech today about what unites us all  it's uplifting to believe that there's  infinitely more that distinguishes and  unites us  because it means that we made conscious  choices in  occupying the spaces that we did  to put this more concretely i want you  to take a moment and think about  who we were as pre-frosh probably  excited  and also hopeful at one of the most  certain and also uncertain points of our  lives  as pre-frosh we had a set of  expectations for what yale would be like  we had ambitions and passions we had  hopes and fears  we came to yale with an established  identity and belief system  standing here today i can tell you that  i failed to meet some of my expectations  i did things that surprised myself i had  incredible experiences and made friends  and memories that i wouldn't trade  for the world but the thing that feels  most personal to tell is that i'm not  the same person i was  four years ago and maybe that's obvious  because because it is obvious but it's  also the most  important thing to reflect on  what stayed since you were a prefrosh  and what hasn't  because of yale i am more in touch with  my heritage  i am less afraid of being judged for  what i like and don't like  i know better what kinds of adventures  i'd find fulfilling  standing here today my identities and  values have been  amplified and altered and challenged and  enriched  because of yale and the experiences i  had  the same is true for all of us  yale gave us more than just access to a  set of traditions and privilege  a claim to the term yeli  yale gave us the opportunity to find a  fuller more authentic version of  ourselves  no matter how long it took for yale to  feel like home  whether you spent your nights piece  setting in bass or dancing in toads or  laughing till your sides hurt in styles  f41  the chosen and eclectic experiences  we've had at this institution  have fundamentally changed who we are  without  compromising who we are  so what's the point  i have no right to stand here and  generalize about all of our collective  experiences  because they were all different and  hopefully they were experiences we  pursued with intention that we had the  agency to choose  and as those experiences tried to change  us hopefully we fought to become better  versions of ourselves  with greater conviction in who we are  and the things we value  so in 15 years when this pandemic is far  behind us  and you're thinking back to your bright  college  years don't just be proud to be a yali  be proud of the person that yale shaped  you to become  thank you

